# Computational irreducibility

**Computational irreducibility** is the property of a computation that has no
substantial shortcut: the only general way to learn its outcome is to carry it
out, step by step. The term is Stephen Wolfram's. In this vault it matters
mostly for what it is not. "Irreducible" was proposed as a mark of a
[[strange-loop]], and as the mark of problems suited to language models, and
in both places the word turned out to cover several different ideas.

## Description

### Three concepts under one word

One of the criteria first proposed for a formal strange loop was that the
system be "irreducible in some sense": it cannot be compressed to rudimentary
computations, yet it can capture the patterns it is trying to describe. The
correction was that irreducibility is ambiguous, and that three concepts have
to be kept apart.

| Concept | What is lacking | About |
|---|---|---|
| Computational irreducibility | A faster way to get the result | The cost of predicting a computation |
| Algorithmic incompressibility | A shorter description of the data | The length of the shortest program that outputs a string |
| [[undecidability]] | Any algorithm at all that always answers | A whole class of questions |

A computation can be irreducible and perfectly decidable: one simply runs it.
A string can be incompressible without any computation being in question.
Gödel's result is about none of these directly. It concerns formal
incompleteness, what a theory can prove, and not a general impossibility of
compression.

### The temptation and its correction

The essay on AI-oriented programming reaches the same word from the other
side. A program solves a problem that is reducible to the algorithm written in
it. A language model is useful exactly when the problem is not fully
specified. The tempting conclusion is:

- classical programming solves reducible problems;
- language models solve irreducible problems.

The essay rejects it on two grounds. A language model is itself a
computational system and does not escape the limits of computation. And
irreducibility has the specific meaning above, which differs from
undecidability, and both differ from a third thing: a problem whose
requirements are ambiguous or incomplete. That third thing, not either of the
formal ones, is where language models earn their keep.

### Two overlapping sets

In place of the opposition the essay puts two sets.

- **P:** problems solvable by explicitly specified programs and algorithms.
- **L:** problems for which generative inference, interpretation or synthesis
  is a useful strategy.

Their intersection is enormous. A model writes the SQL that removes
duplicates, and once the rules are fixed an ordinary program applies them
perfectly. A program calls a model to classify a document. Tasks move from L
toward P as knowledge grows and specifications sharpen. Some problems neither
approach solves reliably, and the formal limits bind both.

### The distinction that survives

What is left is a practical line, not a line between computable and
uncomputable problems:

> executing an established formalization, against discovering or revising the
> formalization itself.

Executing is [[deduction]]: given the rules, the result follows. Discovering
is [[induction]] and [[abduction]]: the rules are what is sought. The essay's
closing question, what is reducible with AI, asks which problems can be moved
from the second activity into the first.

## Relations

- It is not [[undecidability]] {relation: distinguished-from}: no shortcut, against no algorithm.
- It is not the incompressibility discussed under [[compression-and-prediction]] {relation: distinguished-from}: cost of a computation, against length of a description.
- It is not what the [[incompleteness-theorems]] {relation: distinguished-from} establish; they concern provability.
- It was proposed and withdrawn as a criterion for the [[strange-loop]] {relation: contrasts}.
- The side of its surviving distinction that executes a formalization is [[deduction]] {relation: uses}.
- The side that discovers one is [[induction]] {relation: uses}.
- It bears on [[prediction]] {relation: contrasts}: an irreducible process can be predicted only by simulating it.
- It bears on the question [[ai-oriented-language]] {relation: answers}, as the argument against sorting problems into reducible and irreducible.

## Neighbouring concepts

- **[[undecidability]].** The absence of any algorithm that answers every
  instance of a question. It is the strongest of the three limits, and the
  only one tied to Gödel-style arguments.
- **[[compression-and-prediction]].** The link between how well a model
  predicts a sequence and how short a code it yields. Incompressible data is
  data no model predicts better than chance.
- **[[induction]].** Inference from observations to a hypothesis, always paired
  with deduction. It is the name this vault gives to discovering a
  formalization.
- **[[strange-loop]].** A hierarchy of levels crossed and returned through.
  Irreducibility is neither necessary nor sufficient for one.

